WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:03.275
(relaxing dance music)

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:08.610
- All right, I have
good news and bad news.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.760
What do you want first?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.000
The good news, great.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.833
Good call.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:14.973
It's still a Pixel.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.253
The bad news?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.015
It is still a Pixel.

00:00:21.015 --> 00:00:24.098
(lively dance music)

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.540
See, the Pixel 10 has had some high hopes

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.850
and some hype behind it
for the past couple months.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:33.740
You know, it's the 10th generation.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.160
It could be special.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.010
The first one with a TSMC
chip could be special.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.590
Some other things could be
revamped alongside Android 16.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.510
Like, it was looking really promising

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.270
to potentially reboot
the whole Pixel thing,

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.600
to finally have it really
competing with flagships.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.620
Now instead, it is very
much all the things we liked

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.620
and all the things we disliked
about the Pixel already,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.810
but the competing with flagships part

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.540
is coming in the form of a
mainstream marketing push.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.260
A Jimmy Fallon presentation,
a Jonas Brothers music video.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.510
I can't say I would've spent
the money the exact same way

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.160
if I was in charge of the Pixel program,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.590
but hey, that's why I'm the reviewer

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.420
and they're the Google executives.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.080
So, unlike the event, let's
just focus on the phones.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.560
So, this thing is very similar
to last year in design,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.870
in materials, and in character.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.420
Like the camera visor,
the glossy glass back

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.860
with the satin aluminum sides,

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.900
and vice versa on the Pro phones.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.370
Even as an enthusiast about this stuff,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.530
you could probably show me a Pixel 9,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.370
and unless I knew exactly which
one was the new gray color,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.230
it could be easily
mistaken for the new one.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.540
And to me, honestly, that's fine.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.560
I love this design.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.140
The camera bar is nice
for holding the phone.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.460
It gathers some dust, sure,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.740
but you'll probably also
have a case on your phone.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.890
Great, click-y buttons, great haptics.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.830
All of these things are
still true about the Pixel.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.680
The bad news though, is that
the things that the Pixels

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.340
of the past have been lacking,
this one is still lacking.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.260
Pixels have never had the biggest numbers

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.090
or the most bleeding-edge hardware.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.400
And these Pixel 10s are still Pixels,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.040
so they don't have super fast charging.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.280
They don't have a brand new
massive silicon carbon battery.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.590
They don't have huge new camera sensors

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.270
or record-breaking displays.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.670
My personal biggest
annoyance with the Pixel 9

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.680
was the optical under
screen fingerprint reader

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.513
was kinda slow.

00:02:20.513 --> 00:02:23.100
This Pixel 10 actually does have

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.570
an ultrasonic fingerprint reader now,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.160
and it's faster than Pixel 9,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.540
but just a slight beat

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.700
behind the other ultrasonic flagships now.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.530
But that counts as technically new.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.750
So, hey, while we're at
it, here's everything

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.480
that's technically new with the Pixel 10s

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.890
and my take on them.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.730
The main new thing is we
get a full triple cameras

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.010
on the base phone, and I'll
get more into the quality

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.380
of those cameras in a minute,
so that is a nice inclusion.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.850
After that, it's a bunch
of subtle things that sort

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.490
of combine to make it feel
a little more flagship-y

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.650
but without actually leapfrogging

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.390
into ultra phone territory.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.590
So, the fingerprint reader, like I said,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.690
is ultrasonic now and slightly faster.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.030
And the display that it's built into

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.240
is a bit brighter too, so
it looks better outdoors.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.160
The bezels didn't get thinner,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.390
but there's little grills now

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.730
over the slightly better speakers
at the bottom of the phone

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.590
that are color matched to the metal now.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.460
Cool.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.090
And the physical SIM card
tray is now completely gone.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.090
This phone in the US is eSIM
only, which I don't like.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.600
eSIM support, great.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.910
Forcing me to use eSIM?

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:27.840
Not my favorite thing.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.340
Only the carriers win with this.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.230
And to be fair, maybe
I'm over-indexing this

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.270
just a little bit because
I test a lot of phones.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.030
I move my physical SIM card really quickly

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:36.960
between lots of phones.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.640
It takes, like, 10 seconds to switch

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.810
and test something out real quick.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.280
So, I don't like this at
all, but yeah, eSIM only.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.890
The batteries are about
200 milliamp hours larger

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.870
in these phones across the lineup.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:49.770
That's a nice win.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.600
But the internals that are
gonna make a real difference

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.460
that people are actually gonna care about

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.850
are Tensor and Pixel Snap.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.940
So, all right, we gotta talk
about this new Tensor chip.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.010
Google phones, for years,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.110
have used off-the-shelf
chips from Qualcomm,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.580
which made sense, they were great.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.920
And then the Pixel 6 was the first one

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.950
to use their in-house
design Tensor chip instead.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.890
And the promise of Tensor
was never really clear,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.110
but the idea was to give
Google much more control

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.270
over these chips and for optimizing

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.277
for exactly what they
wanna get out of a Pixel.

00:04:20.277 --> 00:04:24.030
The thing is, the first
Tensor chip wasn't that good.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.970
Actually, it was so weak that
it had people considering

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.280
if they should have even left
Qualcomm in the first place.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.360
But, you know, we gotta let 'em cook

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.193
for a little bit, right?

00:04:31.193 --> 00:04:32.850
Let them develop this idea.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.890
And so, a couple generations in,

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.080
all of these first few Tensor chips

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.440
were sort of co-designed
with Google and Samsung,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.140
and it was heavily based
off of an Exynos chip

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.390
and actually still fabricated by Samsung.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.070
So, fast forward to today,

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.760
the Pixel 10 is powered
by a Tensor G5 chip,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.450
the first version of a totally
Google-designed Tensor chip

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.100
on a three-nanometer process

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.470
and manufactured straight by TSMC,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.350
which seems like the perfect opportunity

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:05.490
to make a huge leap in
power, in efficiency,

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.470
in achieving the original mission

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.270
of doing their own Tensor chip,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.510
and it turns out it's good.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.240
It's a really good chip,
but it's not magic.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.210
The main complaint that I and
other Redditors would have

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.620
about Tensor chips of the past

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.160
was that they were simply
not flagship level power

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.050
of CPU and GPU.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.790
And this is $1,000 phone.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.060
Like, for this price, I
could just get a Galaxy S25+

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.560
and have a Snapdragon 8 Elite,

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.960
or an iPhone 16 with an A18.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.130
So, this new Tensor G5 is
more performant than before,

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.890
but it's not miraculously
jumping all the way to the level

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.740
of the Snapdragon 8 Elites of the world.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.330
And I'm starting to think

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.340
that's not even really one of its goals.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.580
Tensor G5 benchmarks
like a mid-range phone,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.970
like the CPU is about 30%
better than last generation,

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.500
but is a solid amount still

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.230
behind the Snapdragon
chips in Samsung phones.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.690
And it's closer to, like, the 8S Gen 4,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.520
the one in the Nothing Phone 3.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.597
And the GPU.

00:06:03.597 --> 00:06:07.620
The GPU gap, if my benchmarks are correct,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.270
are even bigger.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.760
For some reason, Geekbench 6 OpenCL scores

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.730
are literally half of the Pixel 9s

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.350
and a full, like, 600% behind
the Snapdragon 8 Elite.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.183
Jesus.

00:06:20.183 --> 00:06:22.650
Its strongest area though,
is definitely the TPU,

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:26.190
where this one is 60% more
powerful than last generation,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.930
and that is intentional.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.460
This is the Tensor processing unit

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.820
that's specifically designed
to accelerate machine learning

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:36.330
and AI-related tasks like
running on device models,

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.490
editing photos, et cetera.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:42.270
This is what Google has decided
is most important to them

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.950
with these phones and these chips.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.690
They want them to be the fastest

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.660
and most capable AI devices.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.020
So, in the real world,
performance wise, this Pixel 10,

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.360
it is a really smooth
phone with everyday tasks.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.400
I've been flying around Android 16,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.560
multitasking, flying through apps,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.450
high refresh rate is always on.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:01.740
Not even really thinking
about it too much.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.000
It's been very fluid.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.350
I will say though,

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.020
when I went in after a few
days into display settings

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.310
and flipped from High
resolution to Max resolution,

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.360
things sharpened up a bit,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.520
but the phone also did get slightly,

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.440
just barely noticeably, slower.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.090
Just a few dropped frames here and there,

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.070
which I didn't expect.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.110
And I would also not be shocked at all

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.030
if you did head-to-head comparisons

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.560
with graphically intense games,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.690
and frame rate measurements
of the Pixel 10

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:31.200
fall far behind the higher,
more powerful GPU chips.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.300
It also lacks
hardware-accelerated ray tracing.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.070
That's just not the big
strength of this phone.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.120
Never has been.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.050
And it's still a Pixel,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.780
so I'm not expecting it ever will be.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.613
What I can say is,

00:07:40.613 --> 00:07:44.730
at least the battery life
for my use has been solid.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.563
It's been solid.

00:07:45.563 --> 00:07:47.820
I've been mostly testing the Pixel 10 Pro,

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.070
not the XL, so even on
a regular-sized phone,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.550
I'm getting all-day battery
near dead by the end of the day,

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:54.960
but, like, six hours of screen-on time

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.070
and high brightness and all that.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.920
So, it's been decently efficient.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.150
My main concern is now shifting

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.010
to how well this phone will age,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.620
so maybe trying to come
back to this in six months,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.500
one year, two years, just
to see how well this does

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.150
compared to previous Pixels.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.130
But for now, the Tensor chip,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.020
it's not some miraculous savior

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.773
that makes it an ultra
flagship, but good chip.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.473
Good chip.

00:08:18.473 --> 00:08:19.920
You know what's really good, though?

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.120
Pixel Snap.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.760
It sounds silly, but this
has genuinely become one

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.300
of my favorite new features
about the Pixel 10s.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.790
It's just such a quality
of life improvement,

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:33.360
especially as someone who
is a card-carrying member

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.920
of Team No Case.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.530
So, these phones have Qi2
wireless charging now,

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.830
and it's only at 15
watts, or 25 on the XL,

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.080
but it's so incredibly convenient

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.300
to just be able to slap the Pixel onto any

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.550
of my existing MagSafe accessories,

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.370
navigating with the phone
in the dash in the morning,

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.500
dropping the phone on a
wireless charger on my desk

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.820
where they automatically align,

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.770
keeping it topped up on a long travel trip

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.210
with my Ridge power bank.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.650
Even if you aren't someone with a bunch

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.810
of existing MagSafe stuff sitting around,

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.750
it just opens you up to this massive world

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.660
of options for magnetic accessories

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.370
for things you can attach to your phone,

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.380
or things you can attach your phone too.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.540
And I even think this is
actually slightly stronger

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.400
than MagSafe magnets on the iPhone,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.230
at least with the first-party
Google accessories

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:18.810
they've optimized, like
with this Pixel ring stand

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:19.643
I got to try.

00:09:19.643 --> 00:09:21.720
Honestly, this was the perfect
addition to this phone.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:22.830
It's like it was already a phone

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.660
that didn't have very
fast wireless charging,

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.780
and so it still doesn't have
very fast wireless charging,

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.840
but it just gained a ton of functionality.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:31.673
Thumbs up.

00:09:31.673 --> 00:09:34.410
The two things that always
make a Pixel a Pixel though,

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.780
well, that would be the
cameras and the software.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.700
So, I have good news and bad news again.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:39.900
Which do you want first?

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:41.970
The good news.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:43.650
Classic.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.680
Yeah, the software is still awesome.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.440
These phones all ship with
Android 16 out the box,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.380
all of the fun material,
3 Expressive updates,

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:56.160
the fun smart wallpaper cutout
effects, the animations,

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.400
the haptics, the
notification jiggle physics,

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.233
all this stuff.

00:10:00.233 --> 00:10:02.310
There's some weird stuff
that's not all perfect,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.920
like I don't love how massive some

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.720
of the buttons are getting.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.970
Like, okay, maybe the
Alarm app one makes sense.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.410
Fine, make those bigger.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.240
But the Weather app,

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.390
like this list of locations
has such massive gaps now.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:16.950
So much dead white space between them.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.240
Or the Phone app now, too.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.760
They're making these buttons huge.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.110
This new Gmail app,

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.500
these archive buttons
are absolutely massive.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.180
And look at this stopwatch.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.260
Jesus.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.360
But, you know, for the most part,

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.400
I'm loving how colorful and clean

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.720
and fluid everything has been.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.820
And there's so many little
things you can tweak

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.980
and poke around and have fun with.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.660
Trust me, just watch a
few YouTube tutorials

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.430
on how to set up the
perfect quick settings

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.310
or have the ultimate
all-widgets home screen set up.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.960
It's awesome, super flexible.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:46.793
You're welcome.

00:10:46.793 --> 00:10:49.350
And then there's all the
AI stuff on top as well

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.780
because, you know, Google.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.300
And there's a lot of this
stuff that sort of just pops up

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:56.010
as you start using this phone,

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.320
and you can choose to engage with it

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.150
or just kind of ignore
it if you're not into it.

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.570
But any one of these
features could turn out to be

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.420
that awesome thing that
makes you wanna stick

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:09.960
with the Pixel, like Call
Screening or Now Playing.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.850
And so, the big new one on the Pixel 10s

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:13.500
that's got its own section in the settings

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.510
and everything is called Magic Cue.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.120
And this Magic Cue,
it's a very Google thing

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.330
that's supposed to take data
from across your different apps

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:26.130
and services and then surface
them where you need them.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:27.180
At least that's what it's supposed to do.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.360
Now, I was using this phone
and trying to get it to work.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:34.110
Like I was lobbing up perfect
situations for it to kick in

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.690
and read my Gmail or read my calendar,

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.090
and then give me the
perfect text to send back,

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.900
but it rarely seemed to work
exactly the way I wanted it to

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.760
like it did in my original demos.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.080
The thing it did the most often

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:47.640
was it just kept giving me a link

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.830
back to, like, open my calendar

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.630
and check for the information myself,

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:53.160
which is still kinda helpful,

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.980
but only half as helpful as advertised.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.720
I have all the settings turned on,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.320
so it's supposed to be able to
look into any of these apps,

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:05.820
screenshots, email, Messages,
also Keep and Contacts

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.730
and Calendar to return the answer

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.490
to the question pulled
from inside the app.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:14.610
A few times, I did actually
get that to work for me,

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.710
but sometimes it was wrong.

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:20.220
Like I have Andrew asking me
for my flight info next week,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.370
but it's giving me the info to
the flight I have this week,

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.290
and that would be fine
if I could go edit it

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.690
before I send it, but if I click it it,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.790
just sends the whole thing automatically,

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.830
and doesn't also include
some of the information

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:33.720
I would think you want,
like the flight number.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:35.100
I can see the promise here.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.560
It feels like it's still
a work in progress.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.770
The other thing, though, that
I found pretty interesting

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.650
was conversational editing.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.420
It actually is coming to all
phones with Google Photos,

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.490
so it's not just, like,
a specific Pixel thing,

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:48.960
so I won't spend too much time on it,

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:50.280
but it's potentially a game changer

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.790
because it's semantic photo
editing from text prompts,

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.010
so it understands what you're asking for

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.590
and then does the editing for you,

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.420
from simple stuff like, you know,

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.740
brighten up the faces
or remove the road sign,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.140
or even just make this picture better,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.910
which usually does, like, a nice crop

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:10.320
and centers it around the subject

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.820
and evens out the exposure.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.587
But all the way up to
complicated stuff too, like,

00:13:14.587 --> 00:13:16.950
"Add a hot air balloon to the sky."

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:18.180
It'll do that.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:21.870
Or, "Delete the cars
and make the sky bluer."

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.010
And then it does it.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.900
It just does a surprisingly good job

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:26.790
just from hearing what you said.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.290
Google, genuine question,

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:29.673
is video editing next?

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:31.740
Is that even possible?

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:33.090
Should I be concerned?

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:33.933
Excited?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.030
I don't know, we'll see.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.020
There was a couple other things

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:39.690
like the Journal app and NotebookLM,

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.820
which are both pre-installed
on these phones.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:45.480
Also, daily briefs was
next to useless for me.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.310
Like it's supposed to be tailored to me

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.944
and relevant to my day, but yeah,

00:13:49.944 --> 00:13:53.010
that's not really a ton of
useful information here.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.820
But overall, like I said,
the software is really good.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:58.410
And if there's anything that
pops up that you don't like,

00:13:58.410 --> 00:13:59.970
you can literally just ignore it.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.893
So, that is the good news.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:06.000
So then the bad news,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.500
the cameras.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.750
And this is where it kinda
gets a little bit weird.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.820
It's not like they're bad cameras,

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:14.790
but the bad news is that it
does feel like the cameras

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:16.020
are slipping a little bit.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.450
The Pixel has leaned heavily

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.750
on elite software processing for years.

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:24.990
They, surprise, never really
had the biggest sensors

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.350
or the best hardware,
but they pioneered HDR+,

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.120
and the Pixel camera always had this sort

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.730
of confident, contrasty look to it.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:36.090
This generation of phones,
it still has that look,

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.970
but objectively speaking,
like these cameras,

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.230
they just don't stand out from the pack

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:41.280
nearly as much anymore.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.610
And in some ways, they're kind
of falling behind a bit now.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.080
Triple cameras, like I said,

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.030
are awesome to have on the base phone.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.520
So, that 48 megapixel main camera,

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.490
the ultrawide, and the telephoto is a 5x.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.240
And then the Pros have a bigger
50 megapixel main camera,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.460
a bigger, higher resolution ultrawide,

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.690
and a bigger, higher resolution
5x telephoto as well.

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:07.440
So, regular wide shots from
the Pixel 10 and 10 Pro,

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.520
they look great.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:09.840
You know, sure, the
sensor's a little smaller

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:11.550
on the base phone, but
they both do a great job

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:15.300
with sharpness and dynamic
range and lots of detail,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:16.860
and usually pretty great color too.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.060
They have that look.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:19.950
Night Sight is still elite.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:24.750
But past that, it's just
not good at any type of zoom

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.970
like I was hoping it would be.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.980
The ultrawide is okay,

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.630
but usually pretty soft around the edges

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:32.580
with a bit more chromatic aberration

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:33.600
that I'm used to seeing.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.650
And then trying to zoom in was,
like, just surprisingly bad,

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.410
at least compared to other
flagships with decent zoom.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.690
Almost every photo I've taken,

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:46.980
even on these Pros that's
zoomed in, is just soft.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:48.840
Like, colors would be fine
and it's bright enough,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.370
and it's not a terrible image.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.290
Mission still accomplished, I guess.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.440
But, like, this super res
zoom feature on the Pros

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.320
that kicks in for everything past 30x

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.120
really feels like it encourages me

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.910
to take a bunch of Zoom
photos because once I do,

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:05.670
it'll run it through the
processing and sharpen things up

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:07.290
and resolve detail out of thin air

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:09.360
with a little machine learning.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:11.970
But yeah, the results
just weren't that great.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:13.530
I didn't even go all the way to a 100x.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:18.330
All the pictures I'm taking
are, like, 30 to 50x.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:19.710
And they kinda look like they have

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:21.540
a cartoon filter over them,

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.580
even compared to a pretty mediocre zoom

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.170
like the iPhone 16 Pro.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.210
And it gets absolutely smoked

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:29.580
by a good zoom like the S25 Ultra,

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:30.810
even though that's more expensive.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:34.020
Maybe I shouldn't be complaining
about 50x zoom photos,

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:35.100
maybe nobody cares,

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:39.320
but the sweet spot for zoom
is definitely like 5 to 10x,

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.490
or a little past that maybe.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.550
And then the wide photos are
still obviously really good.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.550
It's just the gaps in zoom
performance are a little weird,

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.770
considering how much it
seems to encourage me

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.630
to take zoom photos.

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:53.280
And then there's video performance,

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:56.700
which is still also beat
behind the flagships.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:57.600
If you wanna see samples,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.580
I'm planning on shooting
the next auto-focus video

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:01.620
on the Lucid Gravity on this phone.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:04.740
So, if you care about
either Pixel 10 Pro video

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.770
or the newest, very impressive Lucid SUV,

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:09.180
get subscribed over there.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:10.800
I'll drop a link below the like button.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.690
But yeah, it'll be all the A-roll shots,

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:13.770
and then the B roll will still be

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.570
from the iPhone so you
can compare the two.

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.270
So, the Pixel 10.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:19.320
It's still a Pixel,

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:23.370
it feels like the Pixel is a finished

00:17:23.370 --> 00:17:25.680
slash complete thought for now,

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:27.510
just based on the way Google's acting

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:29.220
and working around this phone.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.220
And they're not making any
big philosophical changes

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.700
to the way they attack
making a Google phone.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:39.090
They're just trying again
to take it to the masses.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:41.400
So, if you didn't like the Pixel last year

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.597
for certain reasons, like, you know,

00:17:43.597 --> 00:17:45.210
"Oh, it's not the best hardware

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.250
or it's not the most powerful,"

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.950
then you still won't like the Pixel.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:52.980
But if you love the Pixel
of previous years 'cause,

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.050
oh, it's this cool design that you like

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:58.560
or it's got great stills
or awesome software,

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.630
then you'll love this Pixel too.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.390
Google's path of trying to bring the Pixel

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.550
to the masses is definitely challenging.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.230
Maybe they really believe in Jimmy Fallon

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.120
and the other celebrity endorsements

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.340
that will be their savior
and finally make it happen.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.350
Me personally, I think the
other half of their strategy

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.440
of, like, directly addressing the masses

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.740
that are on iPhones here and
giving 'em reasons to switch

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:21.390
with, like, Pixel Snap

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:23.910
and other directly
competitive parallel features,

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:25.800
I think that is far more interesting.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.750
And I did just make an
entire video about this,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.430
so I'll link this below if
you haven't seen that yet.

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.260
But the Pixel still being a Pixel

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.840
to me is mostly good news.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.130
I really like the Pixel.

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.010
I'm probably gonna switch to
this Moonstone Pixel 10 Pro

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.440
as my main daily driver phone

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.780
and then I'm probably gonna
switch off of it to something

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.630
with more tempting,
interesting hardware features.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.580
And then I'll probably
switch back when I get bored

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:49.710
with the other weird software experiences

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.090
and I want this feeling again.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:52.770
And then I'll probably
switch off of it again

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:53.910
when it starts aging poorly.

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:54.743
I don't really know.

00:18:54.743 --> 00:18:57.120
The Pixel is right where it's always been.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.880
But you know it's not
where it's always been?

